James inquired whether standard instrumental variable (IV) methods propagate uncertainty from the first stage to the final outcome uncertainty. I confirmed that standard IV/2SLS methods do propagate this uncertainty if the appropriate IV estimator’s standard errors are used. I detailed the distinction between correct 2SLS estimation and incorrect naïve OLS applications, emphasizing the need for proper IV variance calculations that incorporate first-stage estimation uncertainty. I cautioned that with weak instruments, standard errors could be misleading, advocating for weak-instrument-robust methods. The key takeaway was that standard IV methods account for uncertainty, while naïve two-step OLS methods do not.
